its own with full deliverables (c3163fb5: a staged probe, a minimized reproduction, evidence logs, and a findings report). The quiet-vs-stalled procedure misapplied: pgrep -fl glm did not match the process name, and the rule's own warning — \"some quiet runs are healthy; check the CLI's quirk entry before concluding\" — is exactly what I skipped. The round-2 failure was mine, not glm's. (2) The round's findings OVERTURN mine, and the seat verified them by running the minimized reproduction: my round-1 probe was VACUOUS (it never queued the fixture onto the medium — queue-rows count 0 — so \"the evaluator terminates under budget\" described a machine that never executed an instruction); my \"wedge lives in the encoder session layer\" is RETRACTED (kernel-c's semantic path has no encoder session at any commit). The real root cause, glm's and now seat-verified: after the walker cell's legitimate 2M exhaustion, kernel-attempt's catch hands the exception data to evidence-data — dissoc'ing :session but NOT :consumer (the entire advanced machine) — and upstream-record-data's postwalk (requiring-resolves per node, no cycle guard) re-expands it: the repro's arm D measures the walk unfinished at 120s; arm E counts 78,913 unique containers, 3,442 shared revisits, no cycle — exponential shared-revisit expansion. The gate stall is a HOST-side harness defect, not a VM loop, not D2's record construction. D2's actual regression: the WALKER cell exceeds 2M (arm C captured the exhaustion); the semantic cell completes at 1,105,792 steps with the correct outcome (arm B). (3) The findings are committed on hardening3-h3-1 and routed to kimi's gate (session_12e44768) — they change the D2 picture: the evidence-data blowup is a TEST-side fix (memoization/cycle guard, or dissoc :consumer too); D2's walker-over-2M needs the design answer; the architect's three bars stand.\norchestrator-logs/1793385006-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-yang-clojure-track.md:431:Done: (1) The cljrt fix round r2 (muse API, fresh session) delivered 0ecddf87: defect 1 task-lived runtime globals (projection survives into the caller's run; caller-defined names shadow consistently); defect 2 authentic cell-ref admission (source maps like {:type :cell-ref 42} invoke as ordinary data — observed \"Dead or forged cell reference\" pre-fix); defect 3 kind-of classifies admitted foreign cells as functions (typeof py_fn = \"function\", was \"object\"). Each of the three required regressions was observed FAILING before the fix. Round-reported: matrix 13/16/0 (9/11 baseline + 4 new), yang.base 122/0, kondo 0/0. (2) REMOTE suite verification on jupiter at 0ecddf87 (merge-base CURRENT): matrix {:test 13, :pass 16, :fail 0, :error 0} — the first remote attempt's matrix leg output was lost to an over-greedy filter (the yang.base leg passed through); the rerun captured the real output unfiltered: Ran 13 tests containing 16 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors. yang.base: 7 namespaces, 122 tests, 0 failures, 116s. Delegate claims match the remote actuals exactly. (3) The codex re-gate dispatched (read-only, static-only, with the passed-checks list and quoted outputs per the ruling): its job is the fix semantics (globals lifecycle consistency, the authenticity of the cell-ref structural test vs the witness bar, kind-of regression risk), boundary conformance, and the regressions' strength. (4) The D2-arc collab artifacts filed (35aed87f): evidence-walk fix brief+log, the kimi findings gate, the cljrt rounds (base + fix + both dispatch logs), the two authenticity gates, the reviewer gate, the wedge-encoder dispatch log; docs round 2 under general/; credential scan clean (API-key patterns included).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
